1
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:18,810
Hi there, LaserTech and Indian River State College are welcoming you to the lecture 6 of our video series in introduction to photonics.

2
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:23,810
The title of this lecture is Optical Handling, Positioning and Care.

3
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:30,810
This lecture, certain practical aspects of optical components and amounts are going to be presented

4
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:40,810
that are going to give you a certain level of knowledge that is important for any kind of photonics technician that is working in the photonics lab.

5
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:48,810
So we are going to be talking about the construction and use of different mountings for lenses, mirrors and other optical components.

6
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:54,810
We are also going to explain how single and multi-dimensional and translational and rotational stages work.

7
00:00:54,810 --> 00:01:01,810
We are also going to describe how to set up optical systems involving components, optical benches and evaluation free tables.

8
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:11,810
Certain time is going to be spent on elaborating how to inspect surfaces of optical elements for scratches, chips or digs.

9
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:29,810
And a few other things that would give you a reasonable set of skills, at least in the theoretical level, as to what you need to pay attention to and what you need to know about when you are working with different types of components in a photonics lab.

10
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:41,810
We are going to start with the optical bench or rail. This is an important optical mount that can be used, that's being used in almost every photonics lab.

11
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:51,810
This type of optical mount has a long stable base on which optical components can be mounted and can have different cross-sectional shapes.

12
00:01:51,810 --> 00:02:04,810
So it enables different types of optical mounts with the reverse geometrical configuration to be positioned and move along the rail so that along one single rail you can establish a full optical system.

13
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:16,810
What kind of optical components you can mount on these types of rails, all possible optical components such as lenses, mirrors, filters, detectors, etc.

14
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:22,810
Component supports are attached to the rods with adjustable heights and then mounted up to the rail.

15
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:38,810
See on the left hand side you have a cross-sectional view of an optical rail. We can see that down on the bottom we had a specific type of cross-section of a triangular shape.

16
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:50,810
And then up here we have an optical mount where a specific type of component, in this case a lens, is being mounted onto the rod and a specific holder.

17
00:02:50,810 --> 00:03:08,810
And then you can see two locking screws. If these two screws are loosened you can slide this entire vertical optical mount along this rail and position it at a different position along the rail.

18
00:03:08,810 --> 00:03:18,810
The cross-section of an optical rail can be either triangular, double rectangular rail or flatbed. So we are going to describe each of the three briefly.

19
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:28,810
So first, as far as the triangular rail is concerned, it can be made out of different types of metals such as aluminum, cast iron, steel.

20
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:38,810
The triangular rail would have two groups on either side of the rail that are used to guide the carriages and secure them on the side with the locking screws.

21
00:03:38,810 --> 00:03:51,810
So you can see the two V-groups on the left side and one on the left side and one on the right side of this optical rail that are being used to slide this full optical mount that's being shown here.

22
00:03:51,810 --> 00:04:10,810
And you can see that the two locking screws are kind of titling along these two V-groups and establishing the possibility to lock or secure the optical mount with an optical component at a specific position along the rail.

23
00:04:10,810 --> 00:04:16,810
You may also come across double rectangular rails. These are generally made out of aluminum.

24
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:26,810
We have two rails that are parallel to each other with a fixed spacing between them. The length of this type of optical rail can be between 15 centimeters and two meters.

25
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:33,810
So it can be relatively short, but also relatively long. Two meters is approximately about six feet.

26
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:41,810
These types of optical rails may have also slotted mounting holes that would allow bolts to be attached to a flat table.

27
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:48,810
So the carriages have slots which allow the components to move along the rail and can also be fixed at any point with a side screw.

28
00:04:48,810 --> 00:05:00,810
And finally, very often these types of optical rails are going to have some sort of a linear scale that's running along the length of the rail and is used to locate a position of the component.

29
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:11,810
So the scale can be either metric or in inches. And as I said, it is being used to position components at specific distances.

30
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:20,810
So you don't have to use the tape ruler or any other way to measure the distances between the components.

31
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:34,810
The next would be mentioned is so-called flatbed optical bench. This type of optical rail is pretty heavy and it's being used where you would need a maximum stability for the optical system.

32
00:05:34,810 --> 00:05:41,810
So most of the time the flatbed types of optical rails are made out of high grade stainless steel or granite.

33
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:47,810
The rail would have a dovetail cross section along which carriers can be moved and fixed at desired positions.

34
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:56,810
So this type of bench is quite heavy, as I already mentioned, and it's very stable and it's used where you need a maximum stability.

35
00:05:56,810 --> 00:06:09,810
So in these few slides that we just presented, you can get a pretty good feeling about what you may come across in a photonics lab when it's about optical rails.

36
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:23,810
Obviously, you will have different cross sections, you will have different weights and different stabilities, and then also different types of optical rails will cost differently.

37
00:06:23,810 --> 00:06:38,810
So if you are the technician or the person who is going to select and buy or purchase an optical rail for your optical system, you would have to take into account all these different aspects that we were briefly talking about.

38
00:06:39,810 --> 00:06:43,810
Mentioning, including the cost.

39
00:06:43,810 --> 00:06:58,810
So an important aspect of your job would be to have a certain set of skills or knowledge about all the nuances of optical rails that are going to be used in the experiments in your photonics lab.

40
00:06:58,810 --> 00:07:07,810
In addition to optical rails, you are going to use various types of optical tables in the photonics labs.

41
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:13,810
One example of the optical table is shown on this slide.

42
00:07:13,810 --> 00:07:23,810
So we are talking about tables that are used instead of the bench rails where optical elements must be aligned along more than one axis, and when vibration isolation is required.

43
00:07:23,810 --> 00:07:38,810
So if your optical system is going to have a certain 2D type of characteristics, then instead of one dimensional optical rail, you're going to use an optical table.

44
00:07:38,810 --> 00:07:52,810
Optical tables come in different sizes, they can be with and without legs, and most of optical tables would come with drilled or threaded holes that are going to be used to mount different optical components.

45
00:07:52,810 --> 00:08:02,810
These drilled and threaded holes are at certain equal distances from one another so that optical mounts can be directly screwed into them.

46
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:13,810
The optical tables are made out of ferromagnetic stainless steel tops, and they are filled with a honeycomb course and provide flat rigid support for optical elements.

47
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:35,810
If you are tasked to select an optical table, you're going to see that you're going to be faced with a huge selection of different types of tables made out of different materials, different paintings, the size of the threaded holes, the distance.

48
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:51,810
So, for example, distance between the threaded holes can be either one inch or two inches or other distances, and all that is going to impact the cost or the price that you need to pay to purchase that type of optical table.

49
00:08:51,810 --> 00:09:01,810
Another important aspect to mention when we are talking about optical tables is a possibility to isolate from any kind of vibration of the floor.

50
00:09:01,810 --> 00:09:27,810
So, certain experiments that you're going to be performing in a photonic slab, you would want to avoid any kind of external vibrations, and in such a case optical tables with pneumatic isolation legs are used that would basically suppress any kind of vibration of low frequencies below 30 Hz

51
00:09:27,810 --> 00:09:44,810
that are caused by walking on the laboratory floor at similar similar causes. So these pneumatic legs are designed to have vibration frequency far less than 30 Hz so that no vibrations are being transferred to the table.

52
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:58,810
Obviously, these types of tables are much more expensive because they include certain aspects of vibration suppressing on their legs.

53
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:14,810
So, optical tables, again, are an important type of optical mounts that you will be using in your lab, and as already mentioned, they come in different sizes, in different varieties.

54
00:10:14,810 --> 00:10:31,810
There's a huge selection, so the task of selecting an optical table may become pretty enormous if you don't know exactly what you want, so it's very important to know exactly what would be the need of your photonic slab.

55
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:47,810
As already mentioned, it can be just an optical bench with a top, or it can be a full table with the legs, and then very often what you have to take into account is the size of the table.

56
00:10:47,810 --> 00:11:06,810
If you want to use smaller tables, the issue of stability starts playing an important role because the four legs that you're going to put under that table may not provide for stability of the table because you don't want the table to be pushed by the people who are working in the lab.

57
00:11:06,810 --> 00:11:28,810
Tables can also come with a certain shelf if you want to add those extra features, so the price is pretty scalable, and obviously, fancier you go, you will have to pay more for your optical table that you have selected.

58
00:11:29,810 --> 00:11:55,810
Next dimension is different types of supports for lenses and mirrors, so almost any optical system that is to be built in a photonic slab is going to have either a lens or a mirror, so it's very important to have a specific type of support that is going to be used to position and hold in place these types of optical components.

59
00:11:55,810 --> 00:12:12,810
So there's a few different types of mounts used, for example, for lenses. We may have a sliding grip holder, we can have a spring grip holder, a swing arm holder, a threaded holder, a self-centering holder, so many different ways of holding a lens in place.

60
00:12:12,810 --> 00:12:41,810
So here we are just covering these holders in a kind of theoretical way by demonstrating different types through pictures as a part of the hands-on experience that you're going to have at the end of this course, you're going to have a chance to actually work with these supports to hold them in your own hands and mount a few different types of optical systems.

61
00:12:42,810 --> 00:12:57,810
And then perform certain experiments, so at this point it's important to establish certain level of knowledge based on the slides that are going to present it, and then we're also going to have a practical part where you're going to be working with these types of supports.

62
00:12:57,810 --> 00:13:10,810
On this slide we're also going to mention a few other types of supports. On the left hand side you have so-called iris diaphragm, so this type of optical components can produce holes of different sizes.

63
00:13:10,810 --> 00:13:18,810
And then we have different types of holders for filters, either a single filter or a multiple filter, as in the case C.

64
00:13:18,810 --> 00:13:37,810
And then down on the bottom we have a holder for a prism and finally a holder for a laser. So obviously each of these different supports are being used to help the specific optical component in place so that a certain experiment on the optical systems can be performed.

65
00:13:37,810 --> 00:13:51,810
This slide presents two important and very popular types of positioning equipment, especially the one on the top, popularly known as a scissors jack, is extremely popular in photonics labs.

66
00:13:51,810 --> 00:14:04,810
So we are talking about a platform that can be raised up or down or tilted at a certain angle, and these types of platforms would help in adjusting the height of different optical components.

67
00:14:04,810 --> 00:14:26,810
So the scissors jack is nothing else than two heavy metal plates connected by two pairs of crossbars that are hinged at the center, and a scissors jack is going to have a knob that you can turn, and by turning that knob you can either raise the upper horizontal platform or lower the horizontal upper platform,

68
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:34,810
and by doing that you can establish or you can set the position of your component that's being placed on the upper platform.

69
00:14:34,810 --> 00:14:54,810
On the bottom we have a so-called sign table. This type of positioning equipment has two heavy metal plates, one above the other, where the upper plate is attached to the lower plate on the one side by a hinge, while the other side has a screwing mechanism that causes it to move up or down,

70
00:14:54,810 --> 00:15:13,810
and as the screwing mechanism is being used, one side of the heavy plate is moving up and down, as mentioned, and allowing the optical component located on the top plate to be tilted to the desired angle.

71
00:15:13,810 --> 00:15:24,810
So both the scissors jack and sign table are very popular types of positioning equipment, and from these pictures it's pretty self-explanatory how they are working.

72
00:15:24,810 --> 00:15:37,810
You're going to use these types of positioning equipment both as a part of the hands-on experience at the end of this course as well as in a photonics lab, as a photonics technician.

73
00:15:37,810 --> 00:15:53,810
Here we have a few types of translation tables. So transition tables provide adjustable motion in one or two dimensions. They can be combined with other devices to produce either a three-dimensional motion or a combination of linear and rotational motions.

74
00:15:53,810 --> 00:16:10,810
So these translation tables very often would have a micrometer screw incorporated that you can turn, and then by turning that micrometer screw, the top table can move linearly with respect to the bottom part.

75
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:19,810
Very often there's also motion in two axes that can be accomplished by stacking two linear translators above and perpendicular to one another.

76
00:16:19,810 --> 00:16:35,810
You can see that type of a translator on the right-hand side. So the bottom line, you want sometimes to position your optical components on a top surface, and you want to slide it either in one direction or in two perpendicular directions.

77
00:16:35,810 --> 00:16:48,810
And this type of a translation table would enable you that type of flexibility while positioning the optical components in your optical system.

78
00:16:48,810 --> 00:17:00,810
You may also have rotational stages. So in this case, these types of optical mounts would enable a rotational motion about an axis.

79
00:17:00,810 --> 00:17:10,810
The rotary stage is controlled by some sort of a gear on the knob shaft that drives a circular ring of gear through a full 360 degrees.

80
00:17:10,810 --> 00:17:22,810
In this case, these types of devices can be mounted on a vertical shaft to provide rotational motion to an optical component, and very often you're going to see these also incorporated into the translational stages.

81
00:17:22,810 --> 00:17:33,810
So you can have both translational and rotational type of motion of the optical mount that's being mounted onto the platform of these types of stages.

82
00:17:33,810 --> 00:17:46,810
You may also achieve angular motion. In this case, we are talking about two different types of tilt motion. It can either be a single slit tilt or double angle tilt.

83
00:17:46,810 --> 00:17:55,810
So in this case, you have a flexibility to tilt your optical component in either one or two perpendicular directions.

84
00:17:56,810 --> 00:18:09,810
This device is critical in adjusting the position of a laser cavity mirror, for example. So if you're working in a photonics lab that deals with lasers, you may come across this type of angular motion types of holders.

85
00:18:09,810 --> 00:18:21,810
Another interesting mount is so-called a goniometer. This is a device that can rotate an object about a point such that the rotation point is unobstructed by the goniometer itself.

86
00:18:21,810 --> 00:18:39,810
These types of optical mounts would enable you to produce orthogonal motion about a common point in space. So it's also being controlled by two knobs that are positioned perpendicularly.

87
00:18:39,810 --> 00:18:47,810
And it also has certain applications in the photonics lab. Not that popular, but you may come across this type of device as well.

88
00:18:47,810 --> 00:18:53,810
The last part of this lecture is dedicated to the handling and care of optical components.

89
00:18:53,810 --> 00:19:07,810
So it's important to understand that over time, most of the optical components that you're using in a photonics lab are going to develop certain defects either on the surface or inside the bulk material.

90
00:19:07,810 --> 00:19:18,810
So those types of surface defects can include scratches, dicks, chips, and fractures, while an internal defect is usually called an inclusion.

91
00:19:18,810 --> 00:19:27,810
And also these types of defects can also be the defects that have been produced during the manufacturing of these types of optical components.

92
00:19:27,810 --> 00:19:44,810
So you may have defects on the cemented surfaces in multiple lenses, etc. So all this can be caused by mishandling or some sort of mechanical or thermal stress that has been exerted on an optical component.

93
00:19:44,810 --> 00:20:04,810
So once more, you may come across things such as surface abrasion, scratches, dicks, edge chips, cemented surfaces, and all these types of surface defects may affect the performance of optical components in your optical system.

94
00:20:04,810 --> 00:20:14,810
So it's very important to pay close attention to the maintenance and the handling and the care of optical components in the photonics lab.

95
00:20:14,810 --> 00:20:31,810
So as a photonics technician, it's very important and it's crucial to understand that the level of sensitivity to these types of surface defects significantly increases in the photonics lab

96
00:20:31,810 --> 00:20:41,810
compared to other types of electronics labs, because here we are dealing with the light and very often we are dealing with lasers of a high power

97
00:20:41,810 --> 00:20:59,810
where these types of defects can not only degrade the entire optical system that you have built and you are working on, but also can create certain reflections that can be potentially damaging to the electronics within your optical systems.

98
00:20:59,810 --> 00:21:13,810
So it is very important as a photonics technician to know how to inspect various surfaces for any kind of defect and then decide whether or not to use that component in your optical system.

99
00:21:13,810 --> 00:21:19,810
So this slide presents one possible way of observing and measuring the surface defect.

100
00:21:19,810 --> 00:21:36,810
So what you are trying to accomplish here is to observe the surface under the microscope. The way this is being accomplished is by first cleaning the surface of the optical component with and then approve the method and then placing it on a stage of a microscope

101
00:21:36,810 --> 00:21:48,810
and then using a lamp with a condensing lens that would illuminate the test piece and then finally perform the inspection for scratches, dicks and other surface defects.

102
00:21:48,810 --> 00:21:58,810
So if you are looking at the optical element, that optical element in order to be usable should have no scratches on the center of the lens or within the usable aperture of the lens.

103
00:21:58,810 --> 00:22:08,810
So most of the optical beams would be moving exactly to the center of the lens and certain distance away.

104
00:22:08,810 --> 00:22:19,810
So that's going to be the most important and the most critical part of the lens to inspect for any kind of scratches, dicks or other type of surface defects.

105
00:22:19,810 --> 00:22:34,810
There's also other ways to perform the inspection on an optical element. For example, we can use a laser beam that's being passed through a test piece and then you're looking at the output beam from the test piece

106
00:22:34,810 --> 00:22:42,810
that's being diverged by a negative lens and focused on a screen. So this is going to provide a certain amount of magnification.

107
00:22:42,810 --> 00:22:54,810
So you're going to see the projected image that's then to be carefully inspected for any kind of local variation or of brightness that would indicate a possibility of a surface defect.

108
00:22:54,810 --> 00:23:02,810
So to ensure that the observed variations are not due to the properties of the laser or diverging lens, you would want to remove the test object

109
00:23:02,810 --> 00:23:16,810
and then watch the image and it's important for that image to not have any kind of indication of any damage in your laser system or picture that's being produced by the laser system.

110
00:23:16,810 --> 00:23:29,810
Very often you're going to be moving the test piece in two dimensions perpendicular to the laser beam to be able to inspect different portions of the test piece for any kind of surface defect.

111
00:23:30,810 --> 00:23:49,810
Another important test to be performed on optical components is so-called flatness testing. The flatness testing is being performed on a plain surface of a specific type of a component such as an optical window and it can be measured with the help of so-called optical flat.

112
00:23:50,810 --> 00:24:02,810
So the test plate, the optical component that is to be inspected is placed on this so-called optical flat and then viewed under the monochromatic light.

113
00:24:02,810 --> 00:24:12,810
And you are expecting for the fringes or dark and bright bands that appear due to the interference of light reflected from the top surface of the test piece.

114
00:24:13,810 --> 00:24:26,810
So this type of testing is very often being performed in photonic slabs when you are interested in the quality of the flatness of a specific type of optical window.

115
00:24:26,810 --> 00:24:36,810
This light gives you a little bit of insight into what kind of optical fringes you may come across when you're performing a flatness test.

116
00:24:36,810 --> 00:24:41,810
So each of these different patterns indicate something.

117
00:24:41,810 --> 00:24:57,810
So, for example, if you are looking at the fringes on the top right-hand side, you can see certain shapes that would indicate a warp or similar type of a surface defect.

118
00:24:57,810 --> 00:25:00,810
And then also you can see each of these different patterns.

119
00:25:00,810 --> 00:25:16,810
So in other words, the patterns that you are seeing under the monochromatic light would indicate a certain presence of a surface defect that can be correlated to a specific shape of a fringe.

120
00:25:16,810 --> 00:25:22,810
So flatness testing is an important skill for photonics technicians.

121
00:25:22,810 --> 00:25:26,810
So here we are just introducing the whole concept of this test.

122
00:25:26,810 --> 00:25:42,810
And in the future, we are going to be looking deeper into this type of subjects where you're going to learn all the nuances and details of how to correlate a specific shape of a fringe to a specific type of a surface defect.

123
00:25:42,810 --> 00:25:55,810
So with the lecture number six, we are going to conclude our brief introduction into the components, other elements that are being used in the photonics lab.

124
00:25:55,810 --> 00:26:05,810
So after we laid foundation of photonics, after we learned a little bit about the light, important properties of light, terminology of light,

125
00:26:05,810 --> 00:26:12,810
we also spent a few lectures talking about different components that you may come across in the photonics lab.

126
00:26:12,810 --> 00:26:15,810
So we started with optical materials.

127
00:26:15,810 --> 00:26:27,810
We talked about transparent and non-transparent materials, identified a few different types of shapes of optical components, such as a lens, a mirror, optical window.

128
00:26:28,810 --> 00:26:38,810
And we spent some time discussing the optical codings that also provide certain optical performance.

129
00:26:38,810 --> 00:26:46,810
We also spent some time talking about different types of filters that you may come across in a photonics lab.

130
00:26:46,810 --> 00:26:51,810
And finally, in this lecture, we also talked about supporting structures.

131
00:26:51,810 --> 00:27:12,810
So we talked about different types of mounts, rails, and benches that are going to be used to support optical components so that those optical components are aligned in a specific type of optical system that is going to provide a certain application that you are trying to achieve.

132
00:27:12,810 --> 00:27:26,810
And finally, at the end of this lecture, we talked about handling and care of optical components. So that's also an important aspect that a photonics technician should be familiar with.

133
00:27:26,810 --> 00:27:31,810
So this was our brief introduction into the photonics lab.

134
00:27:31,810 --> 00:27:50,810
By having the knowledge presented in these few lectures, you will be able to enter the photonics lab and familiarize yourself further with all these different practical aspects of elements that you're going to be working with in a photonics lab.

135
00:27:50,810 --> 00:28:02,810
With the next lecture, number seven, we are going to shift our attention towards different types of light sources that are used in a photonics lab and in general in photonics.

136
00:28:02,810 --> 00:28:12,810
So we're going to be talking about incandescent fluorescent light sources. We're going to be talking about arc lamps and many other types of our man-bed types of light sources.

137
00:28:12,810 --> 00:28:32,810
And finally, we also have a brief introduction into the anatomy of a human eye so that you understand why it is important from the perspective of our safety to pay a close attention to different types of equipment used in a photonics lab.

138
00:28:32,810 --> 00:28:44,810
At this point, we are going to conclude lecture number six. And as I said, I'm looking forward to seeing you all in the lecture number seven that is going to be covering light sources.
